WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:07.560 --> 00:00:10.086
Hey Wisecrack, Jared here and today we’re
talking the

00:00:10.086 --> 00:00:16.789
ultimate one
hit wonder, - One Punch Man.

00:00:16.789 --> 00:00:19.690
One Punch Man is unlike any anime you’ve
ever seen.

00:00:19.690 --> 00:00:23.610
For one thing, the show parodies just about
every trope in the genre.

00:00:23.610 --> 00:00:27.039
We might examine this in a future episode,
so be on the lookout for that.

00:00:27.039 --> 00:00:31.089
Today, however, we’re looking at another
way the show is unusual: there’s pretty

00:00:31.089 --> 00:00:32.920
much no drama.

00:00:32.920 --> 00:00:36.480
After you’ve seen one or two episodes, you
know how practically all of them are going

00:00:36.480 --> 00:00:39.267
to end: with a big-a** punch and a yawn

00:00:39.267 --> 00:00:41.040
from the
titular One Punch Man.

00:00:41.040 --> 00:00:42.850
So how does the show remain interesting?

00:00:42.850 --> 00:00:45.370
Well, that’s where it gets pretty smart.

00:00:45.370 --> 00:00:50.210
Not only does it provide a hilarious take
on anime but also a more subtle take on where

00:00:50.210 --> 00:00:52.920
humanity is going as a civilization.

00:00:52.920 --> 00:00:56.590
Welcome to this Wisecrack Edition on the Philosophy
of One Punch Man.

00:00:56.590 --> 00:00:58.720
As always, spoilers ahead.

00:00:58.720 --> 00:01:00.580
Our protagonist is Saitama.

00:01:00.580 --> 00:01:04.740
Once a disillusioned young man with a full
head of hair searching for a job in business,

00:01:04.740 --> 00:01:09.400
"I'm no business man, I'm unemployed. Right now I'm looking for a job."

00:01:09.460 --> 00:01:15.540
he discovers a love for hero work that lights
a fire in his eyes.

00:01:15.549 --> 00:01:19.950
But three years later, the thrill is gone,
along with that luscious hair.

00:01:19.950 --> 00:01:24.480
While he claims to be a “hero for fun,”
Saitama doesn’t seem to be enjoying it much.

00:01:24.660 --> 00:01:26.580
"Well, that was lame."

00:01:26.640 --> 00:01:31.200
That’s because he’s become so powerful
that he can defeat any enemy with a single

00:01:31.210 --> 00:01:33.150
swing.

00:01:33.150 --> 00:01:35.149
Ever try to play Mortal Kombat against your
mom?

00:01:35.149 --> 00:01:36.149
It’s not fun.

00:01:36.149 --> 00:01:39.060
That’s what it’s like for Saitama every
time he fights.

00:01:39.060 --> 00:01:41.580
"What the- I didn't even see what happened."

00:01:41.580 --> 00:01:46.420
It would be easy to conclude from Saitama’s
vacant expression and resigned demeanor that

00:01:46.420 --> 00:01:48.249
this is a show about boredom.

00:01:48.249 --> 00:01:50.350
Boredom is certainly a prevalent theme.

00:01:50.350 --> 00:01:55.960
But what the show really focuses on is conflict
- specifically, imagining a world without it.

00:01:56.180 --> 00:02:00.660
In doing so it highlights the importance of
conflict not only to a fictional story, but

00:02:00.670 --> 00:02:02.310
to human experience.

00:02:02.310 --> 00:02:06.719
The show’s abnormal story structure is key
to understanding its message.

00:02:06.719 --> 00:02:11.459
Conventional narratives, whether from a horror
movie, sitcom, or action flick, are driven

00:02:11.459 --> 00:02:12.830
by conflict.

00:02:12.830 --> 00:02:18.800
In his book Story, screenwriting guru Robert
McKee explains: “The music of story is conflict...as

00:02:18.800 --> 00:02:23.790
long as conflict is progressing and building
in the story, it moves forward in time.”

00:02:23.790 --> 00:02:26.810
Without conflict, the characters aren’t
motivated to do anything.

00:02:26.810 --> 00:02:31.230
Thus, in a typical narrative, there’s an
overarching problem that gives the characters

00:02:31.230 --> 00:02:32.380
some purpose.

00:02:32.380 --> 00:02:34.020
Find the bomb before it explodes.

00:02:34.120 --> 00:02:35.320
Survive detention.

00:02:35.440 --> 00:02:37.440
Impress the girl who’s out of your league.

00:02:37.460 --> 00:02:41.220
"Over the course of the story, the heroes
of these tales meet obstacles that force them

00:02:41.280 --> 00:02:42.770
to change and grow.

00:02:42.770 --> 00:02:47.680
Each of these mini-conflicts drives the narrative
forward towards the climax of the story.

00:02:47.680 --> 00:02:52.430
We can illustrate this with countless examples,
but let’s go with a classic: the original

00:02:52.430 --> 00:02:54.110
Star Wars trilogy.

00:02:54.110 --> 00:02:59.360
When we meet Luke Skywalker, he’s an ambitious but inexperienced youth who dreams of adventure.

00:02:59.540 --> 00:03:04.420
He faces constant challenges - the death of
Obi-Wan, the loss of his hand, finding out

00:03:04.420 --> 00:03:06.900
that the hot princess is really his sister.

00:03:06.960 --> 00:03:07.520
"Leia."

00:03:07.820 --> 00:03:12.590
As he overcomes these obstacles, he reaches
greater heights as a character, all in preparation

00:03:12.590 --> 00:03:14.950
for his showdown with Darth Vader.

00:03:14.950 --> 00:03:20.209
This kind of development doesn’t happen
in One Punch Man because, well, Saitama is

00:03:20.209 --> 00:03:21.969
too damn strong.

00:03:21.969 --> 00:03:26.049
Unlike Luke, he decimates any conflict with
one swing of his fist.

00:03:26.049 --> 00:03:31.010
Worse, he knows he can’t lose, so he doesn’t
even benefit from the fear of defeat.

00:03:31.010 --> 00:03:34.370
Absent any adversity, he has nothing to strive
for.

00:03:34.370 --> 00:03:38.450
So, with the exception of a brief flashback
to his origin story, he’s basically the

00:03:38.450 --> 00:03:40.810
same guy throughout the series.

00:03:40.810 --> 00:03:45.280
Even Saitama’s training regiment, usually
an opportunity to depict a character’s mental

00:03:45.280 --> 00:03:50.560
and physical development, is just the same
thing every day - there’s no progression.

00:03:50.900 --> 00:03:57.820
"One hundred push-ups, one hundred sit-ups, and one hundred squats."

00:03:58.160 --> 00:04:02.680
"Then a ten kilometer run. Do it every single day!

00:04:02.880 --> 00:04:07.900
As superhero power sources go, it’s pretty
pedestrian, which appears to foreclose the

00:04:07.909 --> 00:04:10.989
possibility that he has any secrets to reveal.

00:04:10.989 --> 00:04:15.520
Since Saitama is immune to conflict, the bulk
of the episodes don’t really focus on him.

00:04:15.520 --> 00:04:20.500
He’s either not present during most of the
big battles or he’s relegated to a passive observer.

00:04:20.500 --> 00:04:23.000
"You saved me. You're pretty amazing aren't you."

00:04:23.000 --> 00:04:27.800
Instead, they focus on the supporting characters,
establishing their backstories and placing

00:04:27.810 --> 00:04:30.280
them in challenging situations.

00:04:30.280 --> 00:04:34.190
Genos presents a classic character
arc: the wandering warrior with a chip on

00:04:34.190 --> 00:04:38.640
his shoulder convinces a reluctant teacher
to impart secrets of great power,

00:04:38.640 --> 00:04:41.520
"Please teach me the way to become strong like you are."

00:04:41.600 --> 00:04:45.800
also this warrior can tap his inner beast-mode and avenge past injustices.

00:04:45.800 --> 00:04:52.340
"I have an old enemy who must be defeated at all costs. Thought the battle is not for me alone, it is for my entire hometown and productively single as well."

00:04:52.700 --> 00:04:57.900
Think Karate Kid, or the Bride and Pei-Mei
from Kill Bill, or again, Luke Skywalker.

00:04:57.900 --> 00:05:02.840
Even the villains’ motivations are developed
- like Dr. Genus, whose dismissal by the scientific

00:05:02.840 --> 00:05:08.840
community compels him to spend 50 grueling
years trying to unlock the key to humanity's evolution,

00:05:08.860 --> 00:05:14.120
"It's all over, there's no one in this world who can stop my glorious creation."

00:05:14.120 --> 00:05:17.580
all for it to be undercut by one
lackluster punch.

00:05:17.580 --> 00:05:20.980
"I think I'm finished dabbling with evolution."

00:05:21.020 --> 00:05:24.240
Saitama’s motivations, in contrast, are
mundane.

00:05:24.240 --> 00:05:26.360
He’s just doing all this for fun!

00:05:26.360 --> 00:05:28.840
"I'm just a guy who's a hero for the fun of it."

00:05:29.160 --> 00:05:34.380
His powers don’t spring from some deep-seated
pain in his past - he just did a bunch of exercise.

00:05:34.500 --> 00:05:39.540
The ease with which he reached superhero status
robs the supporting characters of any meaning

00:05:39.540 --> 00:05:41.340
they’ve derived from their struggles.

00:05:41.340 --> 00:05:43.880
"I did not ask to study under you to hear jokes!"

00:05:44.000 --> 00:05:46.020
And they get pretty pissed off about it.

00:05:46.020 --> 00:05:53.160
"You say you do this for fun? For fun? How dare you confront Mother Earth's apostle for such a mindless reason."

00:05:53.220 --> 00:05:56.840
Worse, whenever these characters get any kind
of narrative traction,

00:05:56.840 --> 00:06:02.200
"My species won the struggle for survival. We possess the ultimate in regenerative ability."

00:06:02.240 --> 00:06:05.020
Saitama is there to whip the carpet out from under them.

00:06:05.180 --> 00:06:08.280
"Blah, blah, blah. Boring me out of my mind."

00:06:08.560 --> 00:06:13.860
Just when Vaccine Man’s rage is at its peak,
Saitama floors him with one punch, and all

00:06:13.860 --> 00:06:17.889
the dramatic tension built up over the last
several minutes evaporates.

00:06:17.889 --> 00:06:20.639
The same thing happens with Genos in Episode
2.

00:06:20.639 --> 00:06:23.440
As he fights Mosquito Girl we learn about
his character.

00:06:23.440 --> 00:06:27.720
His desire to minimize collateral damage establishes
him as a good guy.

00:06:27.720 --> 00:06:33.360
"When I first spotted her, I confirmed that there were no living beings within 500 meters in all directions."

00:06:33.500 --> 00:06:37.380
He perseveres, even when Mosquito Girl claims
one of his arms.

00:06:37.380 --> 00:06:42.360
Despite the admirable qualities we’ve discovered
in Genos, Mosquito Girl is too powerful for him.

00:06:42.560 --> 00:06:47.860
But Genos has one final move: he plays the
martyr and prepares to self-destruct and save

00:06:47.880 --> 00:06:48.560
the city.

00:06:48.600 --> 00:06:55.760
"I let my guard down, I have no chance of winning, all I can do is self destruct. Forgive me, doctor."

00:06:55.760 --> 00:06:59.880
We’re trained to recognize this as the emotional
climax of the episode.

00:06:59.889 --> 00:07:03.900
But just as he’s charging up for his big
moment, Saitama arrives, and destroys the

00:07:03.900 --> 00:07:07.740
villain with ease, rendering Genos’s struggle irrelevant.

00:07:07.740 --> 00:07:12.379
Every creative writing student learns early
in their education to avoid this kind of narrative

00:07:12.379 --> 00:07:13.379
bail-out.

00:07:13.379 --> 00:07:19.020
It’s called a deus ex machina, a plot device
whereby a character, object, or event swoops

00:07:19.020 --> 00:07:22.330
in to solve a problem that had appeared unsolvable.

00:07:22.330 --> 00:07:27.480
For example, in War of the Worlds, a seemingly
unstoppable alien race decimates the Earth

00:07:27.480 --> 00:07:31.990
for the entire film, only to drop dead en
masse at the very end because they can’t

00:07:31.990 --> 00:07:33.879
handle Earth’s bacteria.

00:07:33.879 --> 00:07:39.199
Saitama is a human deus ex machina - when
the situation is at its most dire, he just pops

00:07:39.199 --> 00:07:43.620
in to save the day with his unbeatable punch
- over and over again.

00:07:43.620 --> 00:07:47.930
When a conflict fizzles out like this, we
don’t only lose the narrative momentum.

00:07:47.930 --> 00:07:51.100
We’re also deprived the opportunity for
catharsis.

00:07:51.100 --> 00:07:56.000
Catharsis is the emotional release of repressed
emotions, and it’s a big part of how we

00:07:56.000 --> 00:07:57.310
relate to stories.

00:07:57.310 --> 00:07:59.449
We connect emotionally to narratives.

00:07:59.449 --> 00:08:03.199
We celebrate the hero’s victories and commiserate
at their defeats.

00:08:03.199 --> 00:08:08.430
By the time we get to the climax of the story,
we are set up for a big emotional payoff.

00:08:08.430 --> 00:08:11.900
Did you tear up when you found out Quill’s
mother called him her little Starlord?

00:08:11.900 --> 00:08:13.680
"My little starlord."

00:08:13.800 --> 00:08:15.900
How about at the end of Toy Story 3?

00:08:15.919 --> 00:08:18.020
Of course you did - you’re not made of stone!

00:08:18.020 --> 00:08:22.580
We respond emotionally because we relate in
some way to the character or the predicament

00:08:22.580 --> 00:08:23.580
they’re in.

00:08:23.580 --> 00:08:26.509
In his essay “Narrating Pain,” philosopher Richard Kearney

00:08:26.509 --> 00:08:30.539
discusses how story permits us to “repeat
the past forward.”

00:08:30.539 --> 00:08:35.880
Through the actions and personas of characters
we can revisit our own lives and past experiences.

00:08:35.880 --> 00:08:40.860
We feel sympathy for Genos, whose sad story
may remind us what it’s like to lose a family member.

00:08:40.860 --> 00:08:43.540
"He even took the lives of everyone in my family."

00:08:43.680 --> 00:08:47.840
Our feelings of encouragement for the Mumen
Rider may stem from our own experience trying

00:08:47.850 --> 00:08:51.529
to measure up to someone that’s bigger,
smarter, or stronger than us.

00:08:51.529 --> 00:08:56.320
We don’t simply feel empathy for these characters,
we’re working out some of our own issues too.

00:08:56.520 --> 00:08:59.720
We see particularly good examples of this
in Japanese media.

00:08:59.720 --> 00:09:04.720
In post WW2 Japan, for instance, the father
of Japanese professional wrestling, a guy

00:09:04.720 --> 00:09:09.510
named Rikidozan, would fight American wrestlers
styled as cheaters and villains.

00:09:09.510 --> 00:09:14.329
Watching Rikidozan struggle, persevere, and
ultimately beat the American fighters allowed

00:09:14.329 --> 00:09:18.340
the Japanese to release some of the emotions
associated with the war.

00:09:18.340 --> 00:09:23.120
To some extent, kaiju movies such as Godzilla,
which depicted mass devastation akin to the

00:09:23.120 --> 00:09:26.920
havoc wreaked by the atomic bomb, served the
same function.

00:09:26.920 --> 00:09:28.760
And it’s not just a Japanese thing.

00:09:28.760 --> 00:09:33.899
Following 9/11, American cinema produced numerous
films that tapped into the emotions associated

00:09:33.900 --> 00:09:35.460
with terrorist attacks.

00:09:35.460 --> 00:09:38.020
One Punch Man is precisely the opposite -

00:09:38.120 --> 00:09:41.640
"My acid breath will melt you into a pile of fleshy pulp."

00:09:44.000 --> 00:09:46.160
it’s the anti-catharsis.

00:09:46.160 --> 00:09:49.860
Sure, it LOOKS like some of those Japanese
kaiju movies on the surface.

00:09:49.870 --> 00:09:54.579
But since Saitama can destroy any enemy with
minimal effort, we don’t feel the drama

00:09:54.579 --> 00:09:59.089
of those threats in the same way Japanese
filmgoers did when they watched Godzilla stomping

00:09:59.089 --> 00:10:00.269
all over Tokyo.

00:10:00.269 --> 00:10:03.660
We just shrug it off, like Saitama does after
destroying the meteor.

00:10:03.660 --> 00:10:09.020
"Stop worrying about it. I think we kept the damage to a minimum. Since no one died we should just call it a win."

00:10:09.120 --> 00:10:13.399
The show has a lot of fun playing the role
of emotional spoiler.

00:10:13.399 --> 00:10:18.529
It goes out of its way to push our buttons,
priming us for a big release...then depriving

00:10:18.529 --> 00:10:19.529
us of it.

00:10:19.529 --> 00:10:23.850
We root for the Mumen Rider because he’s
an earnest underdog, clearly less powerful

00:10:23.850 --> 00:10:26.060
than the big bad S-class heroes.

00:10:26.060 --> 00:10:28.080
I mean he rides a bicycle for god’s sake.

00:10:28.080 --> 00:10:29.920
"The Bicycle is for justice!"

00:10:30.080 --> 00:10:34.279
When he works himself into a lather before
a crowd of cheering onlookers, and attacks

00:10:34.280 --> 00:10:39.280
a powerful foe despite the inevitability of
his failure, we’re supposed to root for him too.

00:10:39.280 --> 00:10:46.060
"It's not about winning or losing, it's about me taking you on right here and right now."

00:10:46.060 --> 00:10:51.640
"You're the cyclist for justice! You can beat him! I believe in you!"

00:10:51.740 --> 00:10:56.360
When the Sea King beats him down, instead
of some recognition of the drama of the situation,

00:10:56.360 --> 00:10:56.860
we get:

00:10:56.860 --> 00:10:59.420
"Nice fight man, you should be proud of yourself."

00:10:59.620 --> 00:11:01.340
Totally anticlimactic.

00:11:01.340 --> 00:11:04.540
Of course, all this is supposed to be funny,
and it is.

00:11:04.550 --> 00:11:07.019
But the show isn’t just playing for laughs.

00:11:07.019 --> 00:11:10.770
It’s highlighting what’s missing in a
life without conflict.

00:11:10.770 --> 00:11:15.980
Japanese theorist Masahiro Morioka has observed
the way our civilization’s drive to minimize

00:11:15.980 --> 00:11:19.820
pain and discomfort has actually drained value
from our lives.

00:11:19.820 --> 00:11:23.160
By and large, life is easier than it’s ever
been.

00:11:23.160 --> 00:11:27.709
On the surface, it may seem that our ability
to reduce pain is good, because it’s enabled

00:11:27.709 --> 00:11:30.150
us to increase our pleasure and comfort.

00:11:30.150 --> 00:11:35.450
But to Morioka, in doing so we gradually “lose
the opportunity of experiencing the joy of

00:11:35.450 --> 00:11:41.040
life that comes from encountering an unwanted
situation and being forced to transform ourselves

00:11:41.040 --> 00:11:44.019
to find a new way of thinking and being…”

00:11:44.019 --> 00:11:48.660
The show provides a great illustration of
Morioka when Saitama recalls his first battle

00:11:48.660 --> 00:11:50.149
against Crablante.

00:11:50.149 --> 00:11:54.579
The win means something to Saitama because
he has to struggle to achieve it.

00:11:54.579 --> 00:11:58.940
Flash-forward three years, and we see Saitama
considering some pre-packaged crab at the

00:11:58.940 --> 00:11:59.940
supermarket.

00:11:59.940 --> 00:12:04.890
This juxtaposition perfectly depicts how modern
life has managed to iron out any hardships.

00:12:04.890 --> 00:12:08.459
Clearly, Saitama would prefer to work for
his seafood.

00:12:08.459 --> 00:12:13.291
Through the lens of Morioka, we see that Saitama’s
frustration stems not only from boredom, but

00:12:13.291 --> 00:12:17.649
from his inability to change and progress
through failure and pain.

00:12:17.649 --> 00:12:22.120
This is clear from the first episode, when
Saitama defeats Vaccine Man and experiences

00:12:22.120 --> 00:12:24.380
not triumph, but frustration.

00:12:24.400 --> 00:12:30.740
"Not again. All it took was one punch. Daammnn itttt!"

00:12:30.820 --> 00:12:35.420
Without any meaningful hardship, his life
has become devoid of purpose.

00:12:35.420 --> 00:12:40.320
Just as a story requires conflict to progress,
so does a human being.

00:12:40.320 --> 00:12:45.290
The idea that ease detracts from life’s
value is articulated again when Saitama reacts

00:12:45.290 --> 00:12:47.040
to the Paradisers’ manifesto.

00:12:47.040 --> 00:12:53.120
"We will continue to riot until food, clothing, and shelter are provided free of charge for those who do not work."

00:12:53.120 --> 00:12:54.380
"Sounds pretty boring."

00:12:54.480 --> 00:12:58.080
For these villains, paradise means success
with no effort.

00:12:58.080 --> 00:13:00.460
Saitama has this, and he thinks it sucks.

00:13:00.460 --> 00:13:02.040
"I've become too strong."

00:13:02.260 --> 00:13:06.920
Morioka agrees, writing that “a civilization
without pain and suffering seems to be the

00:13:06.920 --> 00:13:08.540
ideal of the human race.

00:13:08.540 --> 00:13:13.260
However, I wonder if people might end up with
losing sight of joy, and forgetting the meaning

00:13:13.260 --> 00:13:18.589
of life, in a society pervaded by pain reduction
mechanisms and filled with pleasure.”

00:13:18.589 --> 00:13:23.340
In the last episode of season 1, it appears
that Saitama may have finally met a genuine

00:13:23.340 --> 00:13:26.380
challenger - finally, an enemy who can take
a punch!

00:13:26.380 --> 00:13:27.540
Even multiple punches!

00:13:27.540 --> 00:13:29.560
"Consecutive normal punches."

00:13:29.680 --> 00:13:33.240
But it turns out that Saitama’s been holding
back the whole time.

00:13:33.240 --> 00:13:38.800
"In that case I'll use my own final move. Killer move serious series. Serious punch!

00:13:40.380 --> 00:13:42.500
Yup, life’s still meaningless.

00:13:42.500 --> 00:13:44.940
"You're just too strong."

00:13:45.140 --> 00:13:50.560
And so, in the dead fish stare of Saitama
perhaps we find a warning: don’t get too

00:13:50.570 --> 00:13:51.570
comfortable.

00:13:51.570 --> 00:13:56.140
Be grateful for your failures, and embrace
your pain and suffering, because without it,

00:13:56.160 --> 00:13:57.800
life has no meaning.

00:13:57.840 --> 00:14:04.260
"Oh come on. I'm back to one punch again? Daaamnn ittttt!"

